@startuml
title logGO - Core Interfaces & Structure

interface LogEntry {
  +Timestamp() time.Time
  +Level() Level
  +Message() string
  +Fields() map[string]any
}

interface LogWriter {
  +Write(entry LogEntry) error
  +Close() error
}

interface LogReader {
  +Read(minLevel Level, filter LogFilter) ([]LogEntry, error)
}

interface LogClearer {
  +Clear(before time.Time) error
}

interface LogHandler {
  +Handle(entry LogEntry)
}

class Manager {
  -writer LogWriter
  -reader LogReader
  -clearer LogClearer
  -queue chan LogEntry
  -dropPolicy DropPolicy
  -dropped atomic.Int64
  -handlers []registeredHandler
  +WriteLog(level string, message string, fields ...Field) error
  +WriteEntry(entry LogEntry) error
  +ReadLogs(level string, filter LogFilter) ([]LogEntry, error)
  +ClearLogs(before time.Time) error
  +RegisterLogHandler(handler LogHandler) (unregister func())
  -run()
}

class "file.Store" as FileStore
class "sqlite.Store" as SQLiteStore
class "memory.Store" as MemoryStore

note right of FileStore
  Each backend is ONE type
  satisfying LogWriter + LogReader
  + LogClearer together — not
  separate Writer/Reader types
  per backend.
end note

LogWriter <|.. FileStore
LogReader <|.. FileStore
LogClearer <|.. FileStore
LogWriter <|.. SQLiteStore
LogReader <|.. SQLiteStore
LogClearer <|.. SQLiteStore
LogWriter <|.. MemoryStore
LogReader <|.. MemoryStore
LogClearer <|.. MemoryStore

Manager o-- LogWriter : uses
Manager o-- LogReader : uses
Manager o-- LogClearer : uses
Manager o-- LogHandler : notifies
Manager ..> LogEntry : produces/consumes

note right of Manager
  WriteLog() builds a LogEntry and
  pushes it onto `queue`; a single
  background goroutine (`run`) drains
  the channel, calls writer.Write(),
  then fans the entry out to every
  registered LogHandler — callers
  never block on I/O (the async
  requirement).

  Backpressure is a choice: DropPolicy
  is Block (default: back off the
  caller until there's room) or
  DropNewest (discard + count via
  Dropped()), set via WithDropPolicy.

  Entries are plain values, not
  pooled — an earlier design pooled
  them via sync.Pool, but that's
  unsafe here: entries fan out to an
  arbitrary number of LogHandlers, and
  nothing stops a handler from
  retaining the pointer past its
  Handle() call. The actual low-GC
  requirement is met where the real
  allocation hot spot is instead: the
  file backend pools *bytes.Buffer for
  JSON encoding (no aliasing risk,
  since its contents are fully copied
  out before the buffer returns to the
  pool). See README's "Entries are
  immutable values, not pooled."

  WriteEntry (distinct from WriteLog)
  enqueues an already-built LogEntry
  as-is, preserving its original
  timestamp instead of stamping
  time.Now() — this is what the
  standalone server (04) uses for
  every entry it ingests or receives,
  pulled or pushed.
end note

@enduml
